{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift (roughly 1–2 EUR early horizon, widening to ~5–10 EUR by days 15–30).",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miscalibration: in sustained sell-offs the model expects a rebound/sideways path, but realized prices keep drifting lower. Reduce mean-reversion strength; extend the prevailing downtrend into the forecast path.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: forecast errors grow with horizon (convex error profile). Use an increasing negative correction with lead time (e.g., linear ramp down with horizon).",
    "Peak/upswing overshoot: when the model projects local highs (mid-forecast bumps into mid-70s), realized peaks are lower and occur earlier/are flatter. Damp forecast volatility/amplitude (shrink deviations from a smoothed trend).",
    "Lag in recognizing regime shift: after a break to new lows, the model remains anchored to prior higher range. Re-anchor forecasts to the most recent 5–10 day average and recent slope rather than the 30-day mean."
  ]
}
